{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work","id":1225214,"title":"How do Caucuses Work?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-06-06T04:14:16-07:00","id":1225214,"title":"How do Caucuses Work?","content":"I'm looking for some advice on what's a good way to implement caucuses.<br /> <br /> I understand that the rough idea is that they've been decoupled from consilia and scaled up a bit, but I'm finding that really difficult to work with.<br /> <br /> It works pretty well with the Mysterium and Free Council, but the other three seem heavily predicated on the consilium for defining their limits. Like, the SL book stated that there's a fairly hard limit of one deacon per consilium. There were ways to get more, but essentially the number of offices was tied to the number of consilia.<br /> <br /> However, in 2e, a caucus has a bigger, more ill-defined territory and they overlap. So is it just a case of being powerful enough to have yourself crowned Deacon? Same for the Adamant Sages and Epopts. That could be fun, though it suggests a very Darwinian, almost quasi-feudal society.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, does it make sense for caucuses to simply delineate their territories, so offices are still tied to areas, just bigger areas than consilia?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T06:19:05-07:00","id":1225236,"title":"","content":"There might only be one Deacon per Consilium, but there can be multiple Consilia per Caucus. A Caucus is a a huge, regional swath of territory. Basically, when dealing with the caucus you can scale your problems up beyond the immediate local stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T12:12:28-07:00","id":1225398,"title":"","content":"I'm interested in reading your opinions, too. I'm having trouble picturing how big Caucuses should be and how would they operate across several Consilia in my New Orleans chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:43:33-07:00","id":1225438,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zooroos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1225398#post1225398\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm interested in reading your opinions, too. I'm having trouble picturing how big Caucuses should be and how would they operate across several Consilia in my New Orleans chronicle.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> They way I see it Consilia are like municipal/provincial leadership positions with the Caucus being a &quot;federal&quot; leadership - if that helps any.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11492-arduras","name":"Arduras","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11492_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"206"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T13:55:34-07:00","id":1225448,"title":"","content":"And Caucus territories (From the same or different Orders) can overlap. In that sense it\u2019s like how Comcast and AT&amp;T can operate in the same area. A Consilium serves as an official adjudication hub for problems that crop up between members of different caucuses. I imagine that Caucuses being large enough to accommodate multiple Consilia means that there\u2019s some attempt to do judge shopping and other legal tricks that come from multiple courts being available to any group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T19:58:43-07:00","id":1225515,"title":"","content":"Do we have any examples of overlap and it's boundaries? Going by how it was described i was pretty much got the same vague feeling I got from demon agencies, that they are things but then I have problems actually visualizing how they actually look like because it's so varied in the approaches it can take.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T21:34:32-07:00","id":1225519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iceblade44</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1225515#post1225515\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do we have any examples of overlap and it's boundaries? Going by how it was described i was pretty much got the same vague feeling I got from demon agencies, that they are things but then I have problems actually visualizing how they actually look like because it's so varied in the approaches it can take.</div> </div> </div> </div>I imagine that actual examples won't be out until they finish Tome of the Pentacles, which will be after they do Signs of Sorcery, so who knows how long that will take. Dave has mentioned it before though in posts that I don't currently have the time or resources to dig up.<br /> <br /> Basically though, you can think of a Caucus as big, really big, it has to be in order to find enough Mages to fill an Order's positions, though there never seems to be enough Mages in an Order around to avoid a particular Mage wearing more than a few hats. If we take the U.S.A., we could postulate for instance that the New England area has a bunch of Consilia, one in New York, one in Massachusets in Lovecraft territory, one in Maine dealing with Paul Bunyan type things, etc. The Caucus of a particular Order could cover all of those and maybe some more depending on their influence. More than one Order may be interested in those kinds of Mysteries though, and so you may have multiple Orders that include those Consilia in their areas of interest. The way the Order prioritizes one Consilium over another may end up influencing who's more influential in a given Consilium (Or Assembly because the FC has managed to be a real breakout contender for political power).<br /> <br /> In a way, think of Consilium as Tier 2 from hunter, and Caucus as Tier 3, but they interact in ways that fuzzy the lines between the two, problems can boil down to Tier 2 issues or be blown up to Tier 3 depending on the circumstances. Frankly the math is -really- fuzzy, and it's supposed to be because it depends on the needs of the story, and the inclinations of the ST. You could technically have an apocalypse scenario that's solved at Tier 1 by the cabal of PCs, but you have that option to dragging in so much more from Tier 1 to Tier 4.<br /> <br /> Tier 4 by the way would be the Grand Convocations where multiple Consilia send delegates across Caucus lines to listen to hearings and make collective rulings on truly epic shit, like if an entire legacy should be branded Left-Handed by all Mages, etc.<br /> <br /> I have some things I talked about with a friend of mine, but apparently can't get the color scheming to work with this forum. Sorry guys.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 06-07-2018, 12:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T22:31:25-07:00","id":1225523,"title":"","content":"There's only one Deacon/Epopt/Sage/Hierophant per <b>Caucus</b> in 2e.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-06T22:41:11-07:00","id":1225525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1225523#post1225523\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's only one Deacon/Epopt/Sage/Hierophant per <b>Caucus</b> in 2e.</div> </div> </div> </div>That so? Source?<br /> <br /> Edit: Nevermind, found it on page 70.<br /> <br /> I don't think that detracts from my main point which is that a Caucus' concerns are generally broader than any one Consilium.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 06-07-2018, 01:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T03:54:56-07:00","id":1225539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Do we have any examples of overlap and it's boundaries? </div> </div> </div>Not really. It's more that the Order Status merit mentions it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1225523#post1225523\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's only one Deacon/Epopt/Sage/Hierophant per <b>Caucus</b> in 2e.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's the question more or less. 1e limited the number of caucuses in a way 2e doesn't. So if you don't like the way the Deacon runs things, then it's not obvious what stops you from starting your own caucus (assuming you're powerful enough)? Further, by allowing overlap of caucuses it implies the potential for a level of competition between them that wasn't previously there.<br /> <br /> Basically, how do the Orders counteract the tendency of groups to splinter? If indeed they do? <br /> <br /> As I was writing I realised I hadn't really given any thought to the property of the caucus. That actually could be a interesting way to counter the tendency to splinter. A caucus presumably has some kind of independent legal identity like a cabal so if the Clavinger walks off with half the membership, then they don't necessarily get half the property. Probably have to take it to some kind of arbitration before the Magisters. Now I think about that, that would make a lot of sense with how the Diamond are supposed to work. Starting a new caucus generally means being poor (in magic terms) for a few years.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-07T22:21:45-07:00","id":1225815,"title":"","content":"Caucuses likely define their territory in terms of Consilia, because Consilia are where the largest concentrations of Mages exist, and more importantly, where everything that Mages care about exist. There are probably some grey areas of regions that are loosely considered part of a given Caucus, but few people actually <i>care</i>. There is probably the occasional Hallow where both the local cabal is adequately dedicated to a given Order, and no one finds it interesting enough to turf war over it. There are probably archaeomantic dig sites that various Caucuses argue about jurisdiction over, too, since it's not clear that a Consilium would necessarily form around one.<br /> <br /> It's not dissimilar from how the western US is filled with extremely large tracts of land for states, mostly because it's all just pretty sparsely populated. A Caucus would look something similar: they &quot;claim&quot; a swath of land as large as Alaska, but no one really <i>cares</i> what happens anywhere except Juneau. If a discovery is made deep in the wilderness, they'd probably be able to lend local support until a Consilium forms around that spot, at which point it's pretty obvious that they're taking responsibility for it... or if it's too much trouble, a new Caucus is formally split off to handle it. Sure, people live elsewhere and care about other things, but not enough people do or care for it to be as big a deal.<br /> <br /> More to the point, the Caucus membership has to live <i>somewhere</i>, and they have their own interests beyond claiming territory. The Hierophant is probably studying a particular Mystery in addition to all the mystery cult paperwork being signed off on. Probably doesn't have as much as time as he'd like for that study, and probably has to travel around a lot, but at some point, he comes home and settles in for a good Obsession.<br /> <br /> I'd expect offices to be filled in the same way that offices are always filled: someone wants that kind of position, or everyone else feels strongly enough that they're a good fit that they get pushed in. Absent some explicitly defined system, that's just sort of how things happen.<br /> <br /> That's all the peaceful answer, obviously.<br /> <br /> If you get pissed off at the leadership, there isn't really a higher power to consult. The Caucus leadership is functionally the highest authority within the Order. Granted, that leadership is balanced against the leadership of any Consilia in the area, so if a Caucus runs things badly, Consilia in their jurisdiction might just start kicking out members of their Order and telling them to go elsewhere. Splitting off a new Caucus could easily be the kind of compromise action where the crappy Deacon gets to keep his title, but people don't have to deal with him and no one's really screwed over too badly in practice. (Which is not to say that the Ladder itself would stand for such crappy leadership, but my hypothetical has to stop somewhere.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T04:01:47-07:00","id":1226422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Caucuses likely define their territory in terms of Consilia, because Consilia are where the largest concentrations of Mages exist, and more importantly, where everything that Mages care about exist. </div> </div> </div>That seems to be implied, though it leaves overlap. Also, perhaps more importantly, who defines the territory? There's a radical difference between a council of magisters arbitrating a division of territory and leaving it up to individuals to simply set up their caucus as they see fit. Though, even the first case would still leave a very contentious situation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd expect offices to be filled in the same way that offices are always filled: someone wants that kind of position, or everyone else feels strongly enough that they're a good fit that they get pushed in. Absent some explicitly defined system, that's just sort of how things happen. </div> </div> </div>I think that only applies in fairly small organisations with little power. Offices aren't just work, they also provide influence and power too. Your Hierophant for example, has de facto control over promotion in the Caucus. They stand to influence the shape of the caucus for a good while. If they're an Archivist, then maybe they're less quick to promote Archaeomancers for example. So over time, the caucus becomes dominated by the Archive. Or maybe they try to be fair and neutral, but the Archaeomancers could still think they're being sidelined.<br /> <br /> The point being, it's not just ambition driving that kind of politics (which I think the Order books tended to emphasise), but a kind of zero-sum situation. If your Hierophant doesn't exert their influence, then they're leaving the door open to someone else doing so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1225815#post1225815\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you get pissed off at the leadership, there isn't really a higher power to consult. The Caucus leadership is functionally the highest authority within the Order.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, there's the wider community of Magisters. They don't seem to have formal power to remove people, but a mage only rises to that position if the existing magisters are okay with them, and the consilia already functions on the principle of social pressure and exclusion. The regional Magisters could just recognise and support a new deacon over the old one. It straddles both editions, but that seems to be what happens to Boston. Khumeia deposes the Nemean using the backing of the other Deacons.<br /> <br /> That's more or less where my issue is coming from. In 1e, the consilia gave fairly hard limits to intra-order conflict. Magisters might have differences, but being separated meant I could see them being far less influenced by the kind of factional politics I described above. <br /> <br /> One other thing, I'm assuming that overlap is both common and that all Orders behave more or less the same. Thinking back to the Order books, it was suggested that the Guardians and the Arrow were more or less okay with multiple caucuses, meanwhile the Thearchs were hyper-competitive and would almost always attempt to assimilate any other caucus. That could be the answer there I guess; the SL just don't do overlap, and when they do, it results in conflict of some kind until one wins out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-11T21:33:26-07:00","id":1226679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226422#post1226422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That seems to be implied, though it leaves overlap. Also, perhaps more importantly, who defines the territory? There's a radical difference between a council of magisters arbitrating a division of territory and leaving it up to individuals to simply set up their caucus as they see fit. Though, even the first case would still leave a very contentious situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that both are available as part of the toolbox. I.e., it's fine to build your world either way, and they both work at an abstract level. (Whether they work for your table is, obviously, a more specific question only you can answer.) I do think the idea of a tribunal created by a Convocation to arbitrate inter-Caucus disputes is kinda interesting and could definitely be a neat addition to how a particular table imagines their world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226422#post1226422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that only applies in fairly small organisations with little power. Offices aren't just work, they also provide influence and power too.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's basically what I meant with my reference to the Hierophant having to travel all the time. He's traveling because he has to go to different Consilia and be the Hierophant there, which means deciding Mysterium disputes, deciding on &quot;promotions&quot; (I have no idea what that means in Mage), distributing resources, ordering specific projects or agendas get the go-ahead, etc. Or he makes everyone else travel to him. Or he holds telepathic conferences. Or whatever. The point is just that, while he's doing Hierophant things, he took the position for one essential Mage reason: being the Hierophant gives him access, security, and resources for understanding his personal Obsession. He might get that stuff because he has the authority to <i>take</i> that time, or he might be getting it because, as a figurehead, he's freed from other pressures on his time. Or his position is an expression of his Obsession; maybe he's Hierophant because he's the firmest believer in magic's living nature, or whatever. But I think that's less likely, since it's a political position at the end of the day.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226422#post1226422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, there's the wider community of Magisters. They don't seem to have formal power to remove people, but a mage only rises to that position if the existing magisters are okay with them, and the consilia already functions on the principle of social pressure and exclusion. The regional Magisters could just recognise and support a new deacon over the old one. It straddles both editions, but that seems to be what happens to Boston. Khumeia deposes the Nemean using the backing of the other Deacons.<br /> <br /> That's more or less where my issue is coming from. In 1e, the consilia gave fairly hard limits to intra-order conflict. Magisters might have differences, but being separated meant I could see them being far less influenced by the kind of factional politics I described above.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm honestly a bit confused by this. I think that what you're saying is that a Deacon can maintain power because the neighboring Deacons are less interested in ousting him? Why would they be less interested than before? Why did the regional Magisters care in your example? Why is that missing in the more spread-out 2e land?<br /> <br /> However, I'm going to dispute one of your assumptions:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226422#post1226422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the consilia already functions on the principle of social pressure and exclusion.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that this is the case in 2e. I think that this principle is how a <i>Caucus</i> functions, not a Consilium. A consilium cares about keeping as many of the local Mages alive as possible, and it does that by regulating access to the local major Mystery. That's why it exists: enough Mages congregated, a few people probably came to blows, and a Consilium was established to arbitrate future disputes so that shit doesn't blow up again.<br /> <br /> Taking a step back, for a Mage, their central drive is access and understanding of a Mystery. In order to understand a Mystery, they need Mana, which comes primarily from Hallows. In order to have access to a Mystery, they basically need to not-die while studying it. Some of that access also is predicated on a better understanding of Arcana, which needs a teacher for a reasonable pace of education.<br /> <br /> So there are three major resources underlying the political makeup of Mage: Hallows, which drive the formation of cabals; peaceful dispute resolution, which drives the formation of consilia; and stockpiles of knowledge, which drive the formation of caucuses. Obviously, each of these things can overlap. The physical security of an Athaeneum is probably aided by local Sentinels under orders of a Consilium as much as it's guarded by Mysterium members living in the city: that's probably a basic agreement between the local Consilium with the regional Mysterium Caucus. The Consilium might specify that the Athaeneum isn't allowed to hand out resources to anyone who's pissed off the Consilium somehow, including Mystagogues, and that might be an interesting twist to explore in a story: why did that come about, what circumstances suggest it should change, etc. In return, maybe the Consilium agrees not to grant Hallows to any cabals who have pissed off the Caucus.<br /> <br /> I'm not saying that there [i]isn't/i] any social-pressure-and-exclusion in a Consilium, but it's much less about access to resources in general and much more about maintaining local security. In other words, they regulate Hallows mostly because people would fight over Hallows, so having a procedure for deciding who gets one keeps people from fighting. They defend Lorehouses because they don't want the Libertines to decide to call in buddies and leverage the consequent extra pull which might result in a fight. And so on. A Consilium doesn't care so much that a Deacon is being a bad leader so much as they care that the Deacon is riling people up and making it more likely a fight will break out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226422#post1226422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\">One other thing, I'm assuming that overlap is both common and that all Orders behave more or less the same. Thinking back to the Order books, it was suggested that the Guardians and the Arrow were more or less okay with multiple caucuses, meanwhile the Thearchs were hyper-competitive and would almost always attempt to assimilate any other caucus. That could be the answer there I guess; the SL just don't do overlap, and when they do, it results in conflict of some kind until one wins out.</span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Also, yeah, I'm also treating the Orders as interchangeable here. Because there's definitely no reason to involve the Adamantine &quot;Existence is War&quot; Arrow in this discussion. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T05:31:42-07:00","id":1226741,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think that both are available as part of the toolbox. I.e., it's fine to build your world either way, and they both work at an abstract level. (Whether they work for your table is, obviously, a more specific question only you can answer.) I do think the idea of a tribunal created by a Convocation to arbitrate inter-Caucus disputes is kinda interesting and could definitely be a neat addition to how a particular table imagines their world. </div> </div> </div>Could do a mix actually. I mean, it sets up a situation where there's a formal system, but an incentive to cheat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226679#post1226679\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">deciding on &quot;promotions&quot; (I have no idea what that means in Mage),</div> </div> </div> </div>I was just meaning the progression through the Mystery. The Mysterium book mentions that the Order varies the difficulty of the task you need to accomplish to advance. So, you can be fast-tracked to Hierophant status or left as a neokoros.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm honestly a bit confused by this. I think that what you're saying is that a Deacon can maintain power because the neighboring Deacons are less interested in ousting him? Why would they be less interested than before? Why did the regional Magisters care in your example? Why is that missing in the more spread-out 2e land? </div> </div> </div>Sorry, I meant the other way around. In 1e, where caucuses were confined to consilia, there was little room for overlap of authority between different magisters. The Nemean was the Deacon of Boston, so if you were a thearch and you set up shop in Boston, he'd be your Deacon now.<br /> <br /> In 2e, that's no longer so clear cut. You could be under the authority of the Deacon of New York for example. You now have potentially two different caucuses operating in the same cosilium. It leaves the door open for mages to swap sides to get a better deal from their caucus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't think that this is the case in 2e. I think that this principle is how a <i>Caucus</i> functions, not a Consilium. A consilium cares about keeping as many of the local Mages alive as possible, and it does that by regulating access to the local major Mystery. That's why it exists: enough Mages congregated, a few people probably came to blows, and a Consilium was established to arbitrate future disputes so that shit doesn't blow up again. </div> </div> </div>What I was referring to was the basic way the consilium regulates things. At least as it was in Sanctum and Sigil, (and I think 2e implies it still stands), it was by essentially blacklisting people. They don't formally punish a mage, they just make sure everyone knows what the mage did. Also, I don't think Consilia are just about arbitration, there's clearly still an element of general criminal law too. I mean, the narrative running through the core is a murder investigation and there's also stuff like veil violations that need prosecuting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A Consilium doesn't care so much that a Deacon is being a bad leader so much as they care that the Deacon is riling people up and making it more likely a fight will break out. </div> </div> </div>Sorry, I really badly worded that sentence, I meant to draw a comparison between the way consilia use social pressure and the way the wider community of magisters could do similar.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T08:19:24-07:00","id":1226796,"title":"","content":"I'd like to add, Sanctum &amp; Sigil is an excellent source for this conversation. A source that has aged pretty well, IMO, considering how much the gameline has evolved over the years. S&amp;S describes different types of Consilia, from &quot;we merely settle for arbitration&quot; to &quot;thou shalt surrender your personal rights to the allmighty tribunal&quot;. I think is great that it shows the flexibility of the whole Consilium system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T08:29:11-07:00","id":1226797,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zooroos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226796#post1226796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd like to add, Sanctum &amp; Sigil is an excellent source for this conversation. A source that has aged pretty well, IMO, considering how much the gameline has evolved over the years. S&amp;S describes different types of Consilia, from &quot;we merely settle for arbitration&quot; to &quot;thou shalt surrender your personal rights to the allmighty tribunal&quot;. I think is great that it shows the flexibility of the whole Consilium system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Does it talk about Caucuses which are the subject of the thread though?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-06-12T09:40:43-07:00","id":1226818,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1225214-how-do-caucuses-work?p=1226797#post1226797\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Does it talk about Caucuses which are the subject of the thread though?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sadly, no. Or more exactly, it does talk about caucuses, but only in its first edition version, and is not the focus of the book.<br /> <br /> Edit 1: I actually went to the book in order to check if there was a talk about caucuses. The term 'caucus' appears only twice in the book, as examples of the orders interfering with cabals. Weird.<br /> <br /> Edit 2: I feel like I didn't actually answer your question. Although the book does not really talk about caucuses, it does talk a lot about cabals and consilia. And I think Consilia are territorial clusters for the Awakened, so any Caucus should include one or more Consilia, and in fact it should be <b>centered</b> around a particular Consilium, barring exceptions. That way, one could map a Caucus after the Consilia it includes. A sort of reverse-engineering, if you will.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":216,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","user":"Zooroos","date":". 06-12-2018, 02:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}